{
    "title": "Thanksgiving Day, 2025",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 03, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-03/pdf/2025-21910.pdf",
    "summary": "The document is a presidential proclamation declaring November 27, 2025, as Thanksgiving Day and calling on Americans to gather and offer thanks, with a strong emphasis on faith, national history, and gratitude. It links the holiday to earlier proclamations by George Washington and Abraham Lincoln and highlights themes of unity, resilience, and national progress.\n\nThose who value tradition and religious expression may find this message uplifting. Faith communities and people who appreciate public acknowledgments of God in national life may feel seen. The focus on gratitude, family gatherings, and reflection could also offer a moment of unity for many, and supporters of the administration\u2019s view of economic and global progress may welcome the optimistic framing.\n\nOthers may receive it differently. People who prefer a secular approach to government statements, or who hold different beliefs, could feel excluded by the religious language. Communities that view Thanksgiving\u2019s history as complicated, including many Indigenous people, might see the proclamation as overlooking that complexity. Those facing economic hardship or ongoing challenges may find the upbeat claims about recovery and peace out of step with their daily experience. And people who cannot safely or comfortably gather\u2014due to work demands, health, or other limits\u2014may feel the call to assemble does not reflect their circumstances.\n\nOverall, it is a ceremonial message that blends faith, patriotism, and history to mark the holiday, while its tone and emphasis will resonate differently across a diverse country.",
